{
  "id": "29f86507-73e6-5985-9fb2-f359c6af2488",
  "quality_score": 0.91,
  "review_status": "auto-approved",
  "content_json": {
    "id": "tr-b2-participle-aorist",
    "cefr": "B2",
    "title": "Aorist participle -Ar / -Ir / -mAz (adjectival)",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "what-is-it",
        "body": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The aorist participle is a verb stem extended with the suffix "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-Ar / -Ir"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (positive) or "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-mAz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (negative) that functions as a bare adjective directly before a noun. It carries no tense, no person, and no agreement marking — it simply describes a noun by the action or quality associated with the verb. The result is a compound-like noun phrase that Turkish treats as a fixed or semi-fixed collocation: "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "akar su"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " «running water», "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "çıkmaz sokak"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " «dead-end street». Because it is not a finite verb, it cannot take tense or agreement suffixes in this attributive role."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "title": "what is it?"
      },
      {
        "id": "when-to-use-it",
        "body": [
          {
            "kind": "list",
            "items": [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Habitual or inherent property."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " Use the positive aorist participle when the noun is characterised by an ongoing or inherent action: "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "akar su"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " (water that flows — by its nature), "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "çalar saat"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " (an alarm clock — a clock that rings)."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Permanent impossibility or negation."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " Use the negative aorist participle "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "hilite",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "-mAz"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " when the noun is defined by something that cannot or does not happen: "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "çıkmaz sokak"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " (a street you cannot exit), "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "inanılmaz manzara"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " (an unbelievable view — literally «a view that cannot be believed»)."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Fixed and semi-fixed collocations."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " Many aorist participle + noun pairs have become lexicalised. You will encounter them as vocabulary items, but the pattern is still productive for new combinations."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Recognition: the receding suffix -AsI."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " An older suffix "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "hilite",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "-AsI"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " («worthy of / deserving to be») survives in frozen forms such as "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "görülesi yerler"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " (places worth seeing) and the expletive "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "kahrolası"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": ". It is not productive at B2 — learn to recognise it, but do not form new words with it."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "title": "when to use it"
      },
      {
        "id": "formation",
        "body": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The suffix choice follows the same allomorphy rules as the finite aorist. For the positive form, monosyllabic stems with certain consonant endings take "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-Ar"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "; most other stems take "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-Ir / -Ur / -Ür"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (vowel harmony applies). Polysyllabic stems almost always take "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-Ir"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". The negative is always "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "-mAz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (with vowel harmony: -maz / -mez). No personal or agreement suffix is added in the attributive position."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "head": [
              "verb (infinitive)",
              "stem",
              "positive participle",
              "negative participle",
              "meaning"
            ],
            "kind": "conjugation-table",
            "rows": [
              [
                "akmak",
                "ak-",
                "akar",
                "akmaz",
                "flowing / non-flowing"
              ],
              [
                "çalmak",
                "çal-",
                "çalar",
                "çalmaz",
                "ringing / non-ringing"
              ],
              [
                "çıkmak",
                "çık-",
                "çıkar",
                "çıkmaz",
                "exiting / dead-end"
              ],
              [
                "gelmek",
                "gel-",
                "gelir",
                "gelmez",
                "coming / that does not come"
              ],
              [
                "inanmak",
                "inan-",
                "inanır",
                "inanmaz",
                "believing / unbelievable"
              ],
              [
                "görülmek",
                "görül-",
                "görülür",
                "görülmez",
                "being seen / unseen"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "callout",
            "variant": "default",
            "children": [
              {
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "When the participle is used attributively (before a noun), it stands alone — no buffer vowel, no case suffix, no plural. The noun that follows is the head of the phrase and takes any required suffixes: "
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "em",
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "text": "çıkmaz sokak-ta"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": " (in the dead-end street)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "title": "formation"
      },
      {
        "id": "examples-in-context",
        "body": [
          {
            "en": "The running water coming from the mountain was very cold.",
            "kind": "example",
            "note": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "ak- + -Ar → "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "akar"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Monosyllabic stem ending in a consonant; takes -Ar, not -Ir."
              }
            ],
            "target": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Dağdan gelen "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "akar"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " su çok soğuktu."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "en": "That alarm clock wakes me up every morning.",
            "kind": "example",
            "note": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "çal- + -Ar → "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "çalar"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Literally «the clock that rings» — a fully lexicalised collocation."
              }
            ],
            "target": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "O "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "çalar"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " saat her sabah beni uyandırır."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "en": "Don't park the car in that dead-end street.",
            "kind": "example",
            "note": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "çık- + -mAz → "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "çıkmaz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Negative participle: «a street from which one cannot exit»."
              }
            ],
            "target": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Arabayı o "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "çıkmaz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " sokağa park etme."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "en": "We saw an unbelievable view there.",
            "kind": "example",
            "note": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "inan-ıl- (passive stem) + -mAz → "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "inanılmaz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". The passive voice is built into the stem before -mAz is added."
              }
            ],
            "target": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Orada "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "inanılmaz"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " bir manzara gördük."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "en": "The guide told us about the places worth seeing.",
            "kind": "example",
            "note": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "gör-ül- + -AsI → "
              },
              {
                "kind": "em",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "görülesi"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Receding suffix -AsI; recognise it, but do not produce new forms with it."
              }
            ],
            "target": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Rehber bize "
              },
              {
                "kind": "hilite",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "görülesi"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " yerleri anlattı."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "title": "examples in context"
      },
      {
        "id": "common-pitfalls",
        "body": [
          {
            "kind": "list",
            "items": [
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Adding tense or person suffixes to the attributive participle."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " The aorist participle in attributive position is not a finite verb. Writing "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "mono",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "çıkmıyor sokak"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " or "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "mono",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "çıkmayan sokak"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " for «dead-end street» is wrong in this fixed collocation. The correct form is "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "çıkmaz sokak"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " — the bare negative aorist participle, with no further suffixation."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Choosing the wrong allomorph."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " Learners sometimes apply -Ir to monosyllabic stems that require -Ar, producing "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "mono",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "akır su"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " or "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "mono",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "aker su"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " instead of the correct "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "akar su"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": ". The allomorph must match the stem class. When in doubt, check the finite aorist form of the same verb — the participle uses the identical stem + suffix combination."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": [
                    {
                      "kind": "strong",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "Over-producing the receding suffix -AsI."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " Because "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "görülesi"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " and "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "em",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "kahrolası"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " exist, learners sometimes coin new forms such as "
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "mono",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "kind": "text",
                          "text": "yenilesi yemek"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "text": " («a meal worth eating»). This suffix is no longer productive in modern Turkish; native speakers do not form new words with it. Use it only in the frozen expressions you have encountered, and express the «worthy of» meaning through other constructions instead."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "title": "common pitfalls"
      }
    ],
    "subtitle": "Using the aorist stem as a tenseless adjective before a noun"
  }
}